WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.659 --> 00:00:06.149
Black Mirror has emerged as a smartly critical
anthology series about a not-so-distant dystopian

00:00:06.149 --> 00:00:09.019
future, and humans' relationship with technology.

00:00:09.019 --> 00:00:13.040
Most episodes feel like worst-case scenarios
with the ambiance of The Twilight Zone for

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:14.450
the 21st Century.

00:00:14.450 --> 00:00:18.760
Will our future interactions with technology
be uplifting and empowering, or downright

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:19.880
terrifying?

00:00:19.880 --> 00:00:23.820
Scary or not, though, some installments of
Black Mirror are just plain better than others,

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:28.550
so here's a ranking of every episode of the
show so far, from worst to best.

00:00:28.550 --> 00:00:31.050
Do we even need a spoiler warning here?

00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:33.350
"The Waldo Moment"

00:00:33.350 --> 00:00:37.550
When "The Waldo Moment" first hit screens
in 2013, it was met with a pretty resounding

00:00:37.550 --> 00:00:38.570
yawn.

00:00:38.570 --> 00:00:42.739
Centered on an off-the-wall animated character
which uses sarcasm to insert itself into the

00:00:42.739 --> 00:00:48.330
political arena, the title anti-hero was seen
as far too outrageous to ever be taken seriously

00:00:48.330 --> 00:00:49.760
as a public figure.

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:54.350
After the 2016 election cycle in America,
however, Charlie Brooker's story reads as

00:00:54.350 --> 00:00:58.920
almost clairvoyant, since political theater
began to descend into similarly cartoonish

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:00.100
rhetoric and behaviors.

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:02.320
"Mr. Monroe are you addicted?"

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:03.320
"No."

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:07.000
"Sorry, I mean, are you a d---head?"

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:11.000
Despite that unexpected brush with reality,
though, the episode is still one of the show's

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:16.000
least enjoyable segments due to its relatively
tedious pacing, thin character development,

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:17.990
and uninspired dialogue.

00:01:17.990 --> 00:01:19.340
"Crocodile"

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:23.570
If there's any Black Mirror chapter that should
give people pause about the future of crime-fighting

00:01:23.570 --> 00:01:25.410
technology, it's "Crocodile."

00:01:25.410 --> 00:01:30.460
The episode expands on the idea of memories
being accessible to machines by having insurance

00:01:30.460 --> 00:01:35.420
adjusters rifle through peoples' visual register
for details about incidents they've witnessed.

00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:39.700
The concept and devices involved are compelling,
but the leading character -- an architect

00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:42.600
named Mia -- is just a touch too horrible.

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:46.560
What transpires in the episode is a series
of escalating wrongs that reaches territory

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:51.200
so terrible you'll wish you had the ability
to erase your memory of the entire hour you've

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:53.020
just watched.

00:01:53.020 --> 00:01:54.360
"Metalhead"

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:58.400
Not only is "Metalhead" the shortest Black
Mirror episode, but it's also the only one

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:02.720
that's entirely black and white, which really
ramps up the sense of dread and hopelessness

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:04.830
throughout its 41-minute runtime.

00:02:04.830 --> 00:02:09.390
"Metalhead" centers on a bitter chase between
a woman named Bella and a robot dog, trained

00:02:09.390 --> 00:02:13.170
to slaughter people on sight and brimming
with terrifying weaponry.

00:02:13.170 --> 00:02:17.530
But despite its grim mood and action sequences,
there's not a lot of substance here.

00:02:17.530 --> 00:02:21.450
And its connection to the rest of Black Mirror
is tenuous, which makes it one of the least

00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:23.480
meaningful episodes of the series.

00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:24.480
"Arkangel"

00:02:25.750 --> 00:02:30.060
In the age of helicopter parenting and GPS
trackers for kids, "Arkangel" has a pretty

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:31.060
realistic plot.

00:02:31.060 --> 00:02:35.290
A woman signs her kid up for an experimental
monitoring procedure after she goes missing

00:02:35.290 --> 00:02:39.890
for a brief but scary moment, and the device
proves to be helpful in managing her toddler

00:02:39.890 --> 00:02:40.890
days.

00:02:40.890 --> 00:02:44.360
Things change when her daughter enters her
typical teenage rebellion phase, and mommy

00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:48.540
starts invading her privacy in ways only the
device can allow.

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:50.570
What happens from there is pretty predictable.

00:02:50.570 --> 00:02:55.070
For a show that hinges on its surprise factor,
the result is a boring, overwrought PSA on

00:02:55.070 --> 00:02:56.400
aggressive parenting.

00:02:56.400 --> 00:02:58.900
"The National Anthem"

00:02:58.900 --> 00:03:03.530
Black Mirror's inaugural episode centers on
a royal kidnapping, in which the culprits

00:03:03.530 --> 00:03:07.790
demand that the Prime Minister make whoopie
with a pig on live television in order for

00:03:07.790 --> 00:03:10.850
the nation's beloved princess to be released
unharmed.

00:03:10.850 --> 00:03:16.080
The now-legendary episode offers some serious
cultural commentary on the psychological nature

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:19.810
of viral videos and how the freedom of the
internet challenges authority.

00:03:19.810 --> 00:03:25.500
Oddly enough, the episode also drew some all-too-real
parallels to the supposed PigGate controversy

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:28.030
involving then-Prime Minister David Cameron.

00:03:28.030 --> 00:03:31.990
But in the grand landscape of Black Mirror
now, it's a pretty mediocre episode compared

00:03:31.990 --> 00:03:33.140
to its successors.

00:03:33.140 --> 00:03:35.760
"Shut Up And Dance"

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:40.520
Webcam hacking is a legitimate concern for
the modern age, so the bare bones basis of

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:42.040
"Shut Up and Dance" is solid.

00:03:42.040 --> 00:03:45.860
But the more and more "Shut Up and Dance"
unravels, the less compelling it turns out

00:03:45.860 --> 00:03:46.860
to be.

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:50.420
The narrative starts out with a kid being
blackmailed by people who've recorded him

00:03:50.420 --> 00:03:52.740
having private times at his computer.

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:56.830
He's then sent on a series of increasingly
dangerous missions, and we're left to wonder

00:03:56.830 --> 00:04:00.160
why he'd put himself in so much danger over
something so innocent.

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:04.060
It's not until we find out that his act was
not so innocent after all that things start

00:04:04.060 --> 00:04:05.150
to make sense.

00:04:05.150 --> 00:04:09.069
But by then it's a little too late to redeem
the episode from being boring — the worst

00:04:09.069 --> 00:04:11.050
sin of all.

00:04:11.050 --> 00:04:12.370
"Black Museum"

00:04:12.370 --> 00:04:16.489
In an episode obviously built for Easter egg
hunters, "Black Museum" has a little bit of

00:04:16.489 --> 00:04:21.410
everything: a doctor who gets addicted to
patient pain zaps; a comatose mom whose soul

00:04:21.410 --> 00:04:23.560
gets left to rot in a stuffed animal …

00:04:23.560 --> 00:04:25.340
"Monkey needs a hug."

00:04:25.340 --> 00:04:30.120
...and a hologram soul who's made to experience
his execution for a crime he didn't commit

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:33.740
a thousand times over for the sake of people's'
sick pleasure.

00:04:33.740 --> 00:04:37.170
The anthology-within-an-anthology style of
the episode wasn't new ground.

00:04:37.170 --> 00:04:41.400
But there are enough winks to iconic Black
Mirror moments and surprise twists to give

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:43.850
the audience a minor thrill or two.

00:04:43.850 --> 00:04:45.780
"Men Against Fire"

00:04:45.780 --> 00:04:50.570
The most poignant commentary on the morality
of war comes by way of "Men Against Fire,"

00:04:50.570 --> 00:04:54.060
which centers on a man who belongs to a military
of the future.

00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:58.690
He's got an implant that turns soldiers into
massacre machines, augmenting reality and

00:04:58.690 --> 00:05:03.110
keeping them focused on the task at hand:
wiping out "roaches," which are said to be

00:05:03.110 --> 00:05:05.590
mutated humans who roam the world.

00:05:05.590 --> 00:05:10.210
After a few successful slaughter sessions,
a glitch in his implant reveals the truth;

00:05:10.210 --> 00:05:13.210
these roaches aren't mutants, but ordinary
humans.

00:05:13.210 --> 00:05:16.770
The device has altered their appearance to
make them easier to attack.

00:05:16.770 --> 00:05:21.180
It's a devastating truth, and asks a lot of
questions real-life soldiers' face on any

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:22.530
given battlefield.

00:05:22.530 --> 00:05:25.090
"The Entire History of You"

00:05:25.090 --> 00:05:29.230
Perhaps "Crocodile" and "Arkangel" didn't
work so well because Black Mirror's depiction

00:05:29.230 --> 00:05:33.890
of an Orwellian, tech-enslaved society was
done so well the first time around.

00:05:33.890 --> 00:05:38.610
Capturing every moment, an implant called
a "grain" in "The Entire History of You" allows

00:05:38.610 --> 00:05:42.210
people to replay their memories with all the
ease of Apple TV.

00:05:42.210 --> 00:05:46.210
The vulnerability of the implant is shown
through the eyes of a young lawyer whose growing

00:05:46.210 --> 00:05:51.770
mistrust of his wife leads him down a paranoid,
memory-revisiting tunnel of self-indulgence.

00:05:51.770 --> 00:05:55.700
The story mainly explores how the implant
has fractured social interactions.

00:05:55.700 --> 00:05:59.400
This epitomizes how Black Mirror creates a
version of the near-future that isn't all

00:05:59.400 --> 00:06:03.410
that unrealistic... and all the more terrifying
as a result.

00:06:03.410 --> 00:06:05.150
"White Bear"

00:06:05.150 --> 00:06:09.480
A woman named Victoria Skillane wakes up in
an apartment building with no memory of who

00:06:09.480 --> 00:06:13.979
or where she is, or why people around her
seem more interested in filming her terror

00:06:13.979 --> 00:06:16.979
than helping her escape the weapon-wielding
people who chase her.

00:06:16.979 --> 00:06:17.979
"White Bear!

00:06:17.979 --> 00:06:19.949
There's something not right about White Bear!"

00:06:19.949 --> 00:06:24.540
The episode ends with a massive payoff that
features one morally provoking twist.

00:06:24.540 --> 00:06:29.740
Viewers are left to question everything — from
crime and punishment, to cameraphone culture.

00:06:29.740 --> 00:06:31.919
"Hated in the Nation"

00:06:31.919 --> 00:06:36.750
In this episode, robotic bees have been deployed
to replace real bees' dwindling populations.

00:06:36.750 --> 00:06:41.400
But, of course, the drone devices are hacked
by a rogue group, who turn them against whoever

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:43.880
arrives as enemy number one on social media.

00:06:43.880 --> 00:06:47.520
And then, they amp up the violence even further.

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:52.889
Not only is it a commentary on surveillance,
but it's also a warning signal about the desensitization

00:06:52.889 --> 00:06:57.490
to social media, and the creation of a cyber-mob
mentality.

00:06:57.490 --> 00:06:59.150
"Playtest"

00:06:59.150 --> 00:07:03.290
The all-out horror of this episode begins
when a traveler named Cooper is offered what

00:07:03.290 --> 00:07:07.580
appears to be a dream job — a week's work
for a cutting edge video company.

00:07:07.580 --> 00:07:08.580
The catch?

00:07:08.580 --> 00:07:13.240
A miniature computer implanted into Cooper's
neck, which mixes virtual reality with the

00:07:13.240 --> 00:07:16.690
user's direct experience without the need
for a VR headset.

00:07:16.690 --> 00:07:21.180
The company's technology combines augmented
reality with the ability to scan the brain

00:07:21.180 --> 00:07:23.300
and uncover the user's deepest fear.

00:07:23.300 --> 00:07:29.710
"You just stay in here alone and see how long
you last until you're too scared to continue."

00:07:29.710 --> 00:07:34.650
What ensues is genuinely unsettling, filled
with hallucinations, holograms, and more.

00:07:34.650 --> 00:07:40.070
"Playtest" is an interesting take on the exploitation
of fear as entertainment, how virtual reality

00:07:40.070 --> 00:07:44.949
can theoretically, push this too far, and
the ethical implications we need to consider.

00:07:44.949 --> 00:07:48.720
Will we ever reach a stage when too real is
too much?

00:07:48.720 --> 00:07:50.729
"Hang the DJ"

00:07:50.729 --> 00:07:55.730
The episode begins with a doomed dinner date,
set up by a couple's virtual coach, which

00:07:55.730 --> 00:07:58.139
tells them they'll only be together for half
a day.

00:07:58.139 --> 00:08:01.290
"That's one of the bleakest things I've ever
heard."

00:08:01.290 --> 00:08:05.120
They're separated and thrown into a series
of unsatisfying dates.

00:08:05.120 --> 00:08:09.350
But as they occasionally run into one another
along the way, it's clear they have some legitimate

00:08:09.350 --> 00:08:12.470
chemistry that their little techy tool just
can't understand.

00:08:12.470 --> 00:08:16.470
They're eventually paired up again and agree
to ignore the timer instead of finding out

00:08:16.470 --> 00:08:17.740
their expiration date.

00:08:17.740 --> 00:08:22.140
But when one of them gets anxious and peeks,
it inadvertently speeds up their relationships'

00:08:22.140 --> 00:08:25.919
downfall, which leads them to rebel against
the system altogether.

00:08:25.919 --> 00:08:26.919
The final twist?

00:08:26.919 --> 00:08:30.330
Maybe algorithms do understand love after
all.

00:08:30.330 --> 00:08:31.690
"Nosedive"

00:08:31.690 --> 00:08:36.649
In this slightly enhanced version of things,
every social interaction a person has is given

00:08:36.649 --> 00:08:41.969
a numerical score, and a person's score has
direct impact on what types of housing, finance,

00:08:41.969 --> 00:08:44.759
and job opportunities they have access to.

00:08:44.759 --> 00:08:48.939
The central figure of the story tries reconnecting
with a popular childhood friend to increase

00:08:48.939 --> 00:08:54.339
her ranking, and soon finds that seeking validation
is draining and potentially very damaging.

00:08:54.339 --> 00:08:57.930
So, it's a fair warning to us all, really.

00:08:57.930 --> 00:08:59.480
"USS Callister"

00:08:59.480 --> 00:09:03.819
This episode centers on a man who has invented
a virtual world in which he has a secret sector

00:09:03.819 --> 00:09:07.459
dedicated to his pop culture obsession, Space
Fleet.

00:09:07.459 --> 00:09:12.269
In real life, he's a social pariah in his
own company, but in his world, he's the captain

00:09:12.269 --> 00:09:14.910
and commands complete respect from his crew.

00:09:14.910 --> 00:09:19.370
Too bad his fake world is populated with digital
versions of his real co-workers, and he's

00:09:19.370 --> 00:09:22.360
done some cruel things to them to coax them
into submission.

00:09:22.360 --> 00:09:26.440
After he kidnaps a fellow coder's DNA for
his lair, he meets his match.

00:09:26.440 --> 00:09:28.100
"Okay.

00:09:29.029 --> 00:09:34.440
Stealing my p---- is a red f---ing line."

00:09:34.449 --> 00:09:36.300
That's when the tables turn.

00:09:36.300 --> 00:09:39.740
This sprawling adventure story features massive
set-pieces.

00:09:39.740 --> 00:09:43.889
And even though most of the characters are
digital replicas, they're just as human and

00:09:43.889 --> 00:09:45.559
real as anyone.

00:09:45.559 --> 00:09:47.689
"Fifteen Million Merits"

00:09:47.689 --> 00:09:52.339
This tragic tale centers on a society of people
who earn their merits by cycling on stationary

00:09:52.339 --> 00:09:54.339
bikes to generate energy.

00:09:54.339 --> 00:09:58.339
Merits serve as a currency, but apart from
earning them apples and the right to skip

00:09:58.339 --> 00:10:00.790
lewd commercials, they're not very valuable.

00:10:00.790 --> 00:10:05.649
But having 15 million of them does offer a
person a shot at freedom by way of a ticket

00:10:05.649 --> 00:10:08.869
to a televised talent competition called Hot
Shot.

00:10:08.869 --> 00:10:13.939
Our hero generously decides to gift them to
a girl he barely knows from work in the hopes

00:10:13.939 --> 00:10:16.399
that she can put her singing talents to use.

00:10:16.399 --> 00:10:20.980
But when she's given a gig as an adult film
star, he decides to pedal day and night until

00:10:20.980 --> 00:10:22.740
he can go to the show himself.

00:10:22.740 --> 00:10:24.499
There's no real happy ending here.

00:10:24.499 --> 00:10:29.459
By defeating the system, he becomes just another
cog in another system, with just a slightly

00:10:29.459 --> 00:10:31.189
better bit of window dressing.

00:10:31.189 --> 00:10:35.699
The future is grim, people, and this show's
just here to remind us of that.

00:10:35.699 --> 00:10:37.540
"Be Right Back"

00:10:37.540 --> 00:10:41.850
Losing someone is hard, so when "Be Right
Back" gives a grieving, pregnant woman a chance

00:10:41.850 --> 00:10:46.540
to enjoy more time with her lost love, it's
understandable why she chooses to do so.

00:10:46.540 --> 00:10:51.350
At first, he merely returns to her in chat
format, with a computer program recreating

00:10:51.350 --> 00:10:53.429
his communication style perfectly.

00:10:53.429 --> 00:10:55.709
Then, it becomes more intense.

00:10:55.709 --> 00:11:01.240
Eventually, our heroine realizes her robo-beau
is only a skin-deep replacement for the real

00:11:01.240 --> 00:11:02.240
thing.

00:11:02.240 --> 00:11:05.589
The synthetic version might have gotten his
catch phrases and speaking style down, but

00:11:05.589 --> 00:11:07.819
it's only a fragment of the real person.

00:11:07.819 --> 00:11:09.600
Is that better than nothing?

00:11:09.600 --> 00:11:11.510
"White Christmas"

00:11:11.510 --> 00:11:15.670
This episode begins with a pair of isolated
men, talking with one another on Christmas

00:11:15.670 --> 00:11:16.670
Day.

00:11:16.670 --> 00:11:20.949
One explains how he witnessed the poisoning
of a client he was virtually date-coaching,

00:11:20.949 --> 00:11:25.589
and discusses his day job: creating digitally
cloned consciousnesses.

00:11:25.589 --> 00:11:29.930
The other admits that he'd spent years stalking
his ex-girlfriend and his estranged daughter,

00:11:29.930 --> 00:11:31.189
which didn't end well.

00:11:31.189 --> 00:11:36.000
The final twist reveals a series of shocking
truths, but mostly raises questions about

00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:38.259
the future of the criminal justice system.

00:11:38.259 --> 00:11:42.910
It's another episode that centers on our reliance
on technological evidence, the nature of coerced

00:11:42.910 --> 00:11:47.120
confessions, and has a hint of casual cruelty
in the mix as well.

00:11:47.120 --> 00:11:49.040
It's definitely worth watching.

00:11:49.040 --> 00:11:50.889
"San Junipero"

00:11:50.889 --> 00:11:56.079
As our top episode pick, "San Junipero" shows
love as an all-conquering, blistering light

00:11:56.079 --> 00:11:59.519
that fractures the dark dystopia of the Black
Mirror universe.

00:11:59.519 --> 00:12:03.999
It ultimately illustrates how technology could,
after all, work out for the best.

00:12:03.999 --> 00:12:08.730
The two young ladies in the lead strike up
an unexpected romance in 1987, but then their

00:12:08.730 --> 00:12:11.070
story suddenly takes them through other decades.

00:12:11.070 --> 00:12:15.889
Soon, we discover they're merely projections
of their own consciousness, and their real

00:12:15.889 --> 00:12:19.790
forms are actually in the twilight of their
lives in a hospital.

00:12:19.790 --> 00:12:23.839
What results is an episode that serves as
a meditation on life, the transcendence of

00:12:23.839 --> 00:12:26.870
love, and the power of a great throwback soundtrack.

00:12:26.870 --> 00:12:29.809
"San Junipero" is science fiction at its best.

00:12:29.809 --> 00:12:33.769
If there's any episode from this entire series
that's bound to stick with you and infect

00:12:33.769 --> 00:12:36.369
you with real feelings, this is the one.

00:12:36.369 --> 00:12:37.699
Thanks for watching!

00:12:37.699 --> 00:12:40.290
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:12:40.290 --> 00:12:41.970
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

